  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 934 PM MDT Sun Apr 19 2026\n\nUpper level ridge aloft will slowly progress eastward on Monday.\nWhile doing so, a weak upper level wave will overlay the region.\nThis in combination with anomalous moisture in place will lead to\nslight chances (20-40%) of showers and weak thunderstorms over the\narea Monday afternoon. Dew point depressions will range between\n40-50 degrees, promoting virga with said shower/storm development,\nespecially over the lowlands. That said, gusty outflow winds and\nthe potential for isolated dry lightning will be the main threats.\nHigh temperatures on Monday will be right around the seasonal\naverage.\n\nFor the remainder of the week (Wednesday through Saturday), dry \nsouthwest flow will overlay the region as a slow progressing upper\nlevel system comes onshore over Northern California. This will \nlead to daily afternoon and evening breezy to windy conditions \nthrough the remainder of the week. The increasing PVA and upper \nlevel dynamics will foster lee-side surface low development over \nthe Front Range with surface troughing extending across New \nMexico. That said, at this moment winds each day with remain below\nheadline criteria in terms of wind products. However, elevated to\nnear-critical fire weather conditions will return. Winds will \ngenerally range between 15-25 mph sustained with gusts up to 40 \nmph, with the breeziest days being Wednesday through Saturday.\nHigh temperatures through the week will be above average with\ndaily highs in the middle 80s.",
